 \n- Keep W2 pair, aim for W2 Pung (draw W2).  \n- J3 can become pair with draw J3.  \n\nSo from hand singles: J3, T3, T5, B8 \u2014 which to discard?  \n\nWe must form 2 melds from hand singles:  \nPossibility: (T3, T5, T4) Chow if draw T4.  \n(J3, J3) pair if draw J3 \u2014 then need another meld from remaining singles.  \n\nSo singles after keeping B7 pair and W2 pair: T3, T5, B8, J3 \u2014 that's 4 singles, which with drawn tile can form 2 melds? Not plausible: need 2 chows from those 4 singles with draws, hard.  \n\nAlternative: break W2 pair and use W2 with W3, W4 Chow. But W4 rare (1 left).  \n\nBest discard: B8 seems useless (B6 and B9 dead). T5 also not great but T4 exists. J3 can become dragon Pung (2 fan) if draw 2 more J3 (2 left). T3 can complete Chow with T4, T5 if draw T4.  \n\n**Promising moves**:  \n- Discard B8: keeps B7 pair (potential Pung), keeps W2 pair, keeps T3, T5 for possible Chow (T4 left), keeps J3 for possible dragon pair/Pung.  \n- Discard J3: eliminates dragon possibility, but B8 still dead.  \n\nGiven tile counts:  \nJ3: 2 left, possible to get J3 pair (then J3 Pung needs 2 draws, unlikely).  \n\nBut scoring: Dragon Pung = 2 fan. If I get J3 Pung, that's 2 fan plus other patterns.  \n\nBut easier path: aim for **All Chows** (2 fan) + **No Honors** (1 fan) + **All Simples** (2 fan) + maybe **Self Drawn** (1 fan) = 6 fan only, short of 8.  \n\nSo need higher fan: try for **Half Flush** (6 fan) \u2014 all tiles in one suit plus honors, but I have W, T, B suits in hand, not possible.  \n\nGiven my hand is mixed suits, simplest is to go for ready hand quickly: discard B8 (dead tile), then wait for T4 to complete T3-T4-T5 Chow, and B7 Pung (or J3 pair+Pung).  \n\nAlso, prevailing wind = South (F2), seat wind = East (F1) \u2014 I have neither in hand, so no extra fan there.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation**:  \nPromising moves:  \n1. **Play B8** \u2014 B8 isolated, no Chow possible, B6/B9 dead.  \n2. **Play J3** \u2014 eliminates chance for dragon Pung but dragon Pung needs two more J3, unlikely.  \n3. **Play W2** \u2014 breaks a pair, but W2 has Chow potential with W3/W4.  \n4. **Play T5** \u2014 keeps B8 but B8 dead.  \n\nBest: B8 discard now, then hope to draw T4, B7, or J3.  \n\n---\n\n**Reason**:  \nB8 has no future Chow possibilities given B6 and B9 are exhausted. It\u2019s a terminal-like (but not 1 or 9) but blocking progress. Discarding it keeps flexibility for Chow in T3-T4-T5 (T4 still available) and maintains B7 pair (potential Pung) and W2 pair (potential Pung or Chow). This maximizes chances to form the needed two melds from remaining singles.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer**: Play B8"}
